\section{Service Monitoring and Controlled Interventions}
\subsection{Numerical Perturbation Sensitivity and Response Reuse}
To isolate coordinate sensitivity from live confounders in S1--S2, Table~S8 (Supplement~L) evaluates synthetic in-memory RGB perturbations on B ($40\times 15$) as a controlled numerical continuity stress test under frozen B8v7 cutoffs. All seven raw-score rows keep 0.00\% re-swept EER and 0.00\% frozen FAR (self-centered $\pm 8$ shifts: 1.11\%--2.22\% EER; raw $\Dhybreg{}$ at $-8$: 10.00\% EER). Raw $\Dereg{}$ holds 0.00\% frozen FRR at $\pm 1$ and $\pm 8$ jitter, vs.\ 100.00\% for $\Dhybreg{}$ at $\pm 8$ and 56.00\%--100.00\% under $\pm 15$ jitter, $\pm 8$ shifts, or step-32 channel quantization. As on E (Table~5), centering raises B jitter FRR to 11.00\% ($\pm 8$) and 89.00\% ($\pm 15$).

Because marginal scores depend only on unordered per-cell outputs, zero-entropy screening removes constant centroid replay. Cyclic replay or i.i.d.\ resampling from an exposed reference histogram stays within draw tolerance of $\hat P_{\mathrm{ref}}$, recording 100\% empirical acceptance on B across $\Dhex{}, \Dvox{}, \Drm{}, \Dereg{}, \Dhyb{}$ (Supplement~L). Keeping prompt sets private and rotating them mitigates static lookup tables ($<10{,}000$ tokens/model for a 64-call Dataset~C battery).

